Robot companies usually report how many machines they have built. Far fewer say how much useful work those machines actually do once they are switched on. AGIBOT, a robotics company based in Shanghai, has tried to put a name and a formula on that gap. This post walks through what the company says about its proposed Worken measure and what else it announced the same day, using the company's own release. Everything here is a company statement; nothing in it has been independently confirmed.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Stackbrook did not test any product mentioned and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Worken is, according to AGIBOT

AGIBOT's release is datelined Shanghai, 29 October 2026 (the page metadata shows 28 October), from its Innovation Day event. The company describes Worken as a proposed framework for measuring the productive work robots do in real-world settings. The name combines "work" and "token", and AGIBOT glosses it as "Effective Intelligent Work Hours."

The formula is simple: Worken equals the number of active robots, multiplied by autonomous working hours, multiplied by an output efficiency ratio. Each part is defined in the release as follows. Active robots means units actually performing tasks in real environments, not units manufactured or sitting in inventory. Autonomous working hours counts the time a robot spends working on its own, not the time it is merely powered on. The output efficiency ratio compares what the robot produces with a reference benchmark for a defined task, using a human worker's acceptable output at an accepted standard as one example.

![Flow diagram showing active robots times autonomous working hours times output efficiency ratio equals Worken](/images/site1/agibot-worken-robot-work-metric-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

The wording matters. AGIBOT calls this an evolving framework that it will keep exploring and refining, and says it plans task-specific benchmarks and hopes Worken becomes a widely used reference. Its CTO, Peng Zhihui, is quoted saying Worken is the company's attempt at "a new way of answering that question", meaning how to value robot work. The release also says Worken appears in a 2026 industry report compiled by China Economic Information Service and a Shanghai centre that is part of CAICT. That mention is the company's description; this post did not open the report.

## Why a metric like this is interesting, and its limits

Counting units shipped says little about usefulness, so a measure built on deployed robots, autonomous hours and output quality is a reasonable idea in principle. A buyer comparing robots would likely want exactly those three numbers. But a formula is only as useful as the data behind it, and the release as retrieved publishes none. There is no Worken figure for any AGIBOT robot, no reference benchmark for any task, and no explanation of who would audit the inputs, in the release as retrieved.

The efficiency term is the hardest part. The benchmark of acceptable human work depends on the task, the site and the standard applied, and the release leaves those choices for later. Until benchmarks exist, two companies could report very different Worken numbers for similar work. Because AGIBOT proposed the measure and also sells the robots it would measure, independent definitions and third-party checks would be needed before it could serve as a neutral yardstick. That is a limitation of the proposal as described, not a finding about AGIBOT's robots.

## The products announced alongside it

The release lists several launches. The status words differ, so they are worth separating.

AGIBOT says its G2 Max is launched: a heavy-duty industrial robot with 21 degrees of freedom, dual-arm handling of up to 40 kg and an adjustable working height of 0.1 to 2.5 metres, aimed at manufacturing and logistics. No price or shipping date is given. It also describes AHA3, a third-generation humanoid hardware architecture with Wi-Fi 6 and 5G, joint liquid cooling and IP67-rated joints, as unveiled, again without an availability date. Newly presented items include the OmniHand 3 family of dexterous hands (Lite, standard and Ultra) and the OmniPicker 3 tactile gripper.

On the software side, the company names GO-3 Preview, which it calls a next-generation embodied AI foundation model, and WITA-Omni, an interaction model with full-duplex conversation that, per the release, supports on-device deployment. AGIBOT also highlighted its AGILE motion intelligence framework, which it says now includes a self-evolving agent capability. Link-U OS 1.0, described as released, is billed by AGIBOT as the first operating system built for embodied intelligence. It is being trialled by selected ecosystem partners, and AGIBOT says it plans to make it fully open source by the end of 2026. That date is a plan, not something delivered.

## Claims that rest on AGIBOT alone

The release says GO-3 Preview ranks first on task success rate and process score on RoboDojo, has the highest overall score on RoboTwin and ranks first overall on RoboColiseum. It says WITA-Omni ranks first on multiple benchmarks including Daily-Omni. None of these come with scores, comparison tables or links in the release as retrieved, and GO-3 carries only a "Preview" label with no release date. Treat them as reported company claims, not verified rankings.

The release also cites deployments, including a Longcheer tablet production line, semiconductor packaging and testing, CNC machine tending, logistics, Guangzhou Metro and the Chimelong tourism complex. It gives no output figures for these. It says AGIBOT announced its 20,000th robot in September 2026 and cites 255 universities, 511 enterprises and more than 80,000 developers in its ecosystem. All of these are company-supplied numbers.

## What readers can take from it

For small businesses and everyday readers, this is early-stage news about how robot work might be counted, not a product you can buy today. The practical questions are the ones the release leaves open: what a G2 Max costs and when it ships, whether GO-3 gets a public release, who will check Worken inputs, and whether other makers adopt the idea. This is general information about a company announcement, not purchasing, financial or investment advice.

Two sourcing notes. The pages were read through fetch tools that sometimes return summaries instead of exact text, so the absence of figures, dates or prices below is stated as "in the release as retrieved". The PR Newswire copy was only partly readable, and details from it were used only where they matched the AGIBOT page. No independent coverage or later AGIBOT updates were checked for this post.
